module shared.actors

// The model is an OpenID Provider (OP) and the parties that use it. Sections cited as
// "Core §x" are OpenID Connect Core 1.0; the other specifications are named in full.
//
// Actor or external system: a party is an actor when it calls an endpoint of the OP or
// works on one of its pages (the End-User in the browser, the client software at the token
// endpoint). It is an external system when the OP calls it: the RP's logout and CIBA
// notification endpoints, and the authentication device that CIBA reaches out of band. An
// external system talks to the model only through events and holds no permission.

// Core §1.2: "End-User: Human participant."
actor EndUser "End-User"
  description "Signs in to the OP in a user agent, consents to the claims a client asks for, approves a device or a backchannel request, ends the session, and withdraws a consent."

// Core §1.2: "Relying Party (RP): OAuth 2.0 Client application requiring End-User
// Authentication and Claims from an OpenID Provider." The RP is an actor and not an external
// system because it calls the OP's endpoints: authorization, token, UserInfo, registration,
// device authorization, backchannel authentication, revocation, and end session.
actor RelyingParty "Relying Party (client application)"
  description "Registers itself, sends the End-User to the authorization endpoint, redeems codes, refreshes and revokes tokens, calls UserInfo, polls for device and CIBA tokens, and starts a logout."

// RFC 7662 §1: the protected resource asks the authorization server whether a token is
// active. The model keeps the resource server apart from the RP because it holds no client
// grant of its own; it only asks about tokens.
actor ResourceServer "Resource Server"
  description "Receives access tokens from clients and asks the OP whether each is active before serving the request."

// The model's assumption: an operator registers clients by hand, suspends a misbehaving one,
// and rotates the signing keys. No specification names this role.
actor OpAdministrator "OP Administrator"
  description "Suspends and reinstates clients, publishes, activates, and retires signing keys."

// OpenID Connect Back-Channel Logout 1.0 §2.5: the OP sends a Logout Token to the RP's
// backchannel_logout_uri. CIBA Core 1.0 §10.2 and §10.3: the OP sends the ping or the push to
// the client_notification_endpoint. Core §4: the OP sends the browser to the
// initiate_login_uri. All are endpoints the RP serves and the OP calls.
extsystem RpCallbackEndpoint "RP callback endpoints (backchannel logout, CIBA notification, initiate login)"

// CIBA Core 1.0 §1: "Authentication Device: The device on which the user will authenticate
// and authorize the request, often a smartphone." How the OP reaches it is out of the
// specification's scope (§7.1), so it is an external system that receives a prompt and
// answers with the user's decision.
extsystem AuthenticationDevice "Authentication device (CIBA, out of band)"
